ReDi Yoo et al. (2025) , which rectifies the flow trajectories of continuous token embeddings in Euclidean space. The fundamental difference between their setting and ours lies in the data preprocessing requirement. While their method relies on an external pre-trained model to tokenize the input data, our ReSFlow operates as a fully end-to-end framework directly on the raw data space. By bypassing the discrete tokenization step, our approach is highly advantageous for domains and modalities where well-established pre-trained tokenizers are unavailable. Due to the absence of well-established tokenizers for binarized MNIST and promoter DNA sequences, we restrict our comparison with ReDi solely to the Text8, where characters are tokenized using the GPT-2 tokenizer. We evaluate all methods under different numbers of function evaluations (NFE). For sample-quality metrics such as FID Heusel et al. (2017) and SP-MSE, we report both one-step generation with NFE = 1 =1 and accurate ODE simulation using the adaptive Dormand–Prince 5 (Dopri5) solver Dormand and Prince (1980) . For the Swiss-roll benchmark, we report only Dopri5-based results, including ODE-based NLL and Dopri5 sampling time. Likelihood-based metrics are evaluated only with ODE integration: NLL estimation uses the continuous-time change-of-variables formula and therefore requires accurate integration of the learned dynamics, while a single Euler or exponential-map step does not yield reliable likelihoods. For Text8, BPC is computed from the variational integral formulation rather than generated samples, and is therefore not tied to one-step sampling. Thus, one-step sampling measures fast generation quality, whereas ODE-based integration evaluates NLL and BPC. We also report wall-clock sampling time for binarized MNIST, promoter sequence design, and Swiss-roll to quantify practical runtime , but omit timing for Text8, since the reported BPC metric is based on likelihood evaluation rather than sample generation. 